  bank's 606, none dropped. `meta` now records `base` -- codex ref, ladder ref
  and seed -- alongside `built_from`, where the old bank recorded neither.
  - The self-check inside `rebank.sh` passed: a bank describes the tree that
    wrote it. That was 'different' by construction until today.
  - **The check that matters is cross-tree and it passed too.** A second
    sandbox at the same two refs, bundled independently with its own pwsh and
    its own generator, computed `8632b51e/f4a03c87` -- the banked numbers
    exactly -- and `bank_describes_this_tree` answered `same` about a tree the
    bank had never seen. A fingerprint that quietly encoded the sandbox would
    have passed the self-check and failed this one, which is why both exist.
  - Nit not fixed, deliberately: `meta.base.codex_branch` reads `HEAD` because
    a sandbox worktree is detached. The codex sha beside it is authoritative,
    so this is cosmetic, but "HEAD" reads like a branch name and is not one.
  - The original wording of this item is kept below because the reason it did
    not work is the interesting part. The bank's
  staleness gate is `bank_describes_this_tree`, which compares
  `meta.tools` -- the shas of the two native binaries. Zig bakes the build
  directory into each binary for its stack traces, so the shas move whenever
  the tree does, and every ladder run is in a fresh sandbox by construction.
  Measured: the main checkout's natives carry
  `20260826T160728Z-u50-harness-lift`, and the bank, the main tree and the
  two dup-arms arms have four mutually distinct tool identities. So the
  banner fires on every run whatever the source did, a bank taken today is
  "not about this tree" tomorrow, and the gate can only ever say one thing.
  **The real fix is a source-derived tool identity** -- the bundle
  fingerprint of plug + harness + seed + zig version, which
  `zigc_verify.sh` already computes for its build cache -- or making the
  natives reproducible by keeping the path out of them. Both are infra
  changes; STEVE'S CALL before either is built. Note `meta.base` is worth
  keeping regardless: it records the refs, which is the half that works.

## 2026-08-30 evening -- the first compare_arms artifact, and the corpus rebuilt under it

**`results/8f26755f-vs-58b08c38-core-a456e0cc9414/`** is the run: all five open
PRs plus the arc tangent (branch `all-outbound-u53`, 9 commits) against plain
Update 53, core scope, base `58b08c38` head `8f26755f`, ladder `e9ef9250`.

    stage moves 13, verdict moves 0, zig differs 44 of 1007
    clean 709 -> 726, markers 298 -> 285, population 1229 -> 1233

**Thirteen of the depot's own tests go from `markers` to `clean`** -- emitted
zig carrying `@compileError` never builds, so those thirteen have never run
through this plug. Verdicts moved 0 because only one program in the differing
set was clean-and-runnable on base; the emitted-text comparison is the signal
here, not the verdict.

Per-marker, base -> head: `real-to-int` 17 -> 0, `real-from-int` 15 -> 0,
`real-to-bits` 12 -> 0, `bits-to-real` 5 -> 0, `T27 is not declared` 3 -> 0.
Untouched gaps hold exactly: `poke-32` 140 both sides, `peek-32` 136. Those
counts are now in PRs 100, 105 and 101.

**Four things about the instrument changed today, and each was paid for:**

- **The corpus was 614 programs of 1,704.** `corpus_run.py` globbed
  `codex/test/*.codex` non-recursively behind a comment that had expired --
  the file imports a cite resolver and its own `--all` help says "cites are
  resolved now". Everything under `ops/` was excluded, which is where OUR PR
  EVIDENCE LIVES: `real-bitcast-f64`, `real-int-conversions` and
  `real-saturating-finite`, the control cited in every PR body, had never once
  been swept. Scope is now `core` (1,233) and `all` (1,704), split on measured
  cost -- `apps/` is 28% of the programs and ~96% of the transpile bill.
- **The quire lookup was case-sensitive and PowerShell's is not.** A faithful
  port of `$QuireDirs` into a Python dict silently changed the semantics. The
  depot spells the quire `UI` 493 times and `Ui` 14, two of them inside
  `foreword/ui/`, so the failure reached everything citing those chapters
  transitively. 59 of 1,704 programs unresolved before, 8 after: **51
  recovered, 13 of them in the top-level 614** that every sweep has always
  claimed to cover.
- **`compare_arms.py` replaces the script pair per change.** Three pairs
  existed and all three were buggy in the scaffolding, never in the
  measurement. It refuses when nothing in the corpus can see the change,
  when the two arms share natives while the plug or compiler moved, and when
  a sandbox's `CODEX_ROOT` points outside itself.
- **Transpile everything, run only what differs.** A program whose emitted zig
  is byte-identical across the arms cannot have moved, so 1,185 of 1,233 were
  skipped on a proof. The bitcast run in the morning ran 326 programs per arm
  when 3 files differed.

## 2026-08-30 night -- finding 69 fixed: codexzig reports its diagnostics

`codexzig` discarded every diagnostic that was not an error. It now writes them
to STDOUT, where `CX-DECK` already goes and where the plug's own comment says a
measurement belongs -- stderr carries the emitted zig, so printing a warning the
ordinary way would corrupt the artifact.

**No compiler change was needed.** `write-binary` is the only Codex route to
fd 1 and takes raw bytes; `Foreword chapter CCE` already carries
`text-to-utf8-bytes`, the symmetric partner of `utf8-bytes-to-text`, already in
the bundle. Finding 69 had recorded three ways to do this and all three were
wrong about the cost.

**Three builds, and the second and third are the interesting ones.**

1. CDX0007, unterminated text literal. `czg-halted` ends a line inside a literal
   and closes it with a bare `"` on the next; the same shape with `")` is
   rejected. The line terminator is now the byte 10, appended after conversion,
   which also keeps the accumulator a byte list rather than rebuilding a Text
   per diagnostic (finding 22). **The error gate caught my own broken Codex**,
   which is what it is for.
2. Built green, fixed point held, and it reported NOTHING for a probe the seed
   warns about. The harness merged four bags copied faithfully from the driver
   and **those four were never the driver's list**: `opening.codex:479-482`
   builds `entry-dup-bag`, `shadow-bag` and `collide-bag` itself from
   `scan.def-headers`. **`collide-bag` is CDX3006** -- the diagnostic whose ten
   instances in the safari port started the finding. No reporter could have
   found them.
3. Green, and verified.

**The existing error gate has been incomplete since the day it was added.**
`cdx-duplicate-cite` is `sev-error` and rides in a bag the harness never merged,
so a program with duplicate cites passed the gate and emitted zig.

**Verified** (`findings/f69/verify.sh`): one CDX3005 on stdout for the warning
probe with a program still emitted; nothing for the clean probe; no diagnostic
text in the emitted zig; and the emitted zig for a clean program BYTE-IDENTICAL
to the pre-change binary's, which is what protects safari-web.

**Both harness copies carry it.** `codex-zig-transpiler`'s
`source/CodexZigHarness.codex` is hand-maintained, was committed once at that
repo's skeleton commit, and is what safari runs; it had silently forked. Landed
on branch `harness-notices` (pushed, NOT yet built), and `check_harness_twin.py`
now compares the two copies' definition lines -- prose may differ, code may not.

Also: `ringplug_build.sh` no longer pipes the compiler through
`grep | head`, which had been discarding every failure reason that says neither
"error" nor "SIZE" -- including `compute_lock` refusing beside another session's
guest. Three wrong diagnoses on this day alone.
